    "id": "poem-dt-343",
    "title": "The Story of Purple",
    "subtitle": "The Story of Purple",
    "content": "For nineteen years, I had waited unknowingly — for someone like her. And when she appeared, it was as if my world finally found its missing piece.\nIt all started at the Women's Development Cell stall. There was a Panipuri stall nearby — and that's where I saw her for the first time. She was short, simple, and yet carried an aura that quietly filled the whole place. Something about her presence made the noise around me feel distant, like the world had slowed down just for that moment.\nThen one day, I saw her again — standing right in front of me in a crowd. She turned after buying something, and in that brief, unplanned moment, our eyes met. I was instantly drawn to her. Her beauty had something pure about it, something almost magical — the kind that doesn't demand attention but somehow holds it completely. I couldn't look away. From that day, something inside me quietly shifted, like a door had opened that I never knew existed.\nCuriosity turned into admiration, and admiration slowly, inevitably, became love.\nI found out which class she was in. Days turned into weeks, and somewhere along the way, I finally learned her name . Strangely, I fell in love not just with her, but with her name itself. The way it sounded felt like it belonged to someone extraordinary.\nHer eyes… Those eyes caught mine and held them still. My heart melted like a candle — without a flame. I felt like I was flying without wings, lost endlessly in her gaze.\nShe wore round eyeglasses that only added to her overloaded cuteness. But beyond her looks, what truly captured me was her attitude — calm, confident, and graceful in a way that felt effortless. She never seemed to try too hard. She simply was, and that was more than enough.\nEvery day, my eyes searched for her in every corridor, every crowd, every passing glance. Even when she wasn't around, I could feel her presence lingering — in the spaces between moments, in the quiet corners of my day. She began to live in my thoughts, my dreams, my imagination, like she had always belonged there.\nIn my dreams, I built a small world — just me and her, living quietly in our own space, far from everything loud and complicated. A world where time moved slowly and nothing else mattered.\nMany times, I wanted to talk to her, to tell her how I felt, to let the words I had been holding finally breathe. But every time I gathered the courage to step forward, nervousness pulled me back — gently, stubbornly, like an anchor I couldn't lift.\nUntil one day, I finally spoke to her for the first time — just to wish her a happy birthday. A small sentence. A simple moment. But for me, it was everything. The first time my words actually reached her. The first time my heart finally found its voice.\nHer name stayed with me always, turning over softly in my mind like a quiet song. And so I gave her a nickname — \"Purple.\"\nBecause just like the color, she was rare, deep, and endlessly beautiful. And from that day on, every time I saw the color purple — in a sky, in a flower, in the corner of a painting — I saw only her.",
