    "genre": "Romance",
    "content": "A World Written in Midnight\nThe village was ordinary by day. People worked, ate, slept — no powers, no secrets, nothing strange. But when the sun went down, something appeared above them. A second village, floating in the sky, silent and dark.\nNobody talked about it. There was a rule — after sunset, stay inside. After sunrise, come out. Break the rule, and you were gone from the village forever. No second chances.\nTwo neighbours lived side by side — a boy and a girl. No family, just each other. They got by on small work and quiet days.\nEvery night, the girl broke the rule. She stepped outside and watched the floating village hover above her own. It frightened her, but she couldn't look away.\nThen things started disappearing. First buildings — just gone. Then people. The village leader had no answers. Nobody did. Only questions, hanging in the air like the village above them.\nThe girl said nothing about what she saw. She knew the punishment. But she watched, and she thought, and slowly the fear inside her turned into something else — curiosity.\nOne evening she made her decision. She packed bread, water, and a length of rope. She walked to the edge of the floating village and threw the rope upward. It caught. She climbed — arms burning, hands aching — until she pulled herself over the edge.\nWhat she found stopped her cold.\nEverything was different. The people, the creatures, the buildings — nothing matched the world below. And there was no sun, no moon, no light of any kind. Just thick, absolute darkness. The kind where you couldn't see your own hand.\nShe struck a fire from wood and held it up. The flame carved a small circle of orange in the black. She walked slowly, looking at everything, saying nothing.\nAt the far edge of the village, her foot slipped.\nShe fell — and grabbed the side of a mountain jutting out beneath the village's floor. She hung there, breathing hard. And below her feet, half-hidden in shadow, something old waited. She couldn't make it out clearly.\nShe began to move toward it.\nShe climbed down carefully, the fire-stick tucked in her belt, casting a thin glow against the stone. As she got closer, she saw it clearly for the first time.\nA door.\nOld, carved into the mountain itself. Symbols covered every edge — not letters, not numbers, something older than both. She pressed her hand against it and felt it hum, like something alive was breathing just behind it.\nShe pushed. It opened.\nInside was a room, small and circular. In the centre sat an old woman, cross-legged, eyes closed. A single flame burned above her open palm without burning her skin. Around her, carved into the walls, were images — the village below, the village above, people disappearing, buildings fading. The whole story, told in stone, long before it happened.\nThe girl stood still.\nThe old woman opened her eyes.\n\"You took longer than I expected,\" she said quietly.\n\"You knew I was coming?\"\n\"I knew someone would come. It had to be someone who watched without speaking. Someone who carried the truth inside and chose understanding over fear.\"\nThe girl looked at the walls. \"What is this place? Why are people disappearing?\"\nThe old woman stood slowly. \"Two villages were never meant to exist together. Long ago, one world was split into two — day and night, light and dark. But the split was never finished. So every night, the upper world pulls at the lower one. Taking pieces back. It will not stop until one world remains.\"\n\"Which one survives?\"\n\"That depends on you.\"\nThe old woman showed her two stones, resting on a carved shelf. One white, smooth, warm to the touch. One black, rough, and cold.\n\"Place the white stone below the lower village. The night world dissolves forever. Your people keep their lives, their homes, their ordinary days.\"\n\"And the upper world?\"\n\"Gone. Everything here — the creatures, the people, the darkness — all of it ends.\"\nThe girl looked around. The people here had not chosen this. They were not evil. They were simply trapped on the other side of something broken.\n\"And the black stone?\"\n\"Both worlds merge. Day and night become one. Nobody disappears. But the world that forms — nobody knows what it looks like. Not even me.\"\nThe girl held both stones for a long time.\nShe thought about the boy back home. About the empty houses, the missing faces, the fear in the village every morning. She thought about the creatures she had seen up here — strange, quiet, moving through the dark like they had always known nothing else.\nShe put the white stone in her pocket.\nShe placed the black stone on the ground.\nAnd she walked back to the rope and climbed down.\nWhen her feet touched the ground below, dawn was an hour away. She walked to the centre of the village, knelt down, and pressed the white stone into the earth.\nThe ground hummed. The floating village above trembled. Its edges softened like smoke at the end of a fire. Slowly, silently, it came apart — piece by piece dissolving into the sky until there was nothing above them but open air and early stars.\nNo crash. No sound. Just absence.\nMorning came. The sun rose normally. People stepped outside and looked up without knowing what had been there. The buildings that had vanished were still gone — some things the dark had already taken could not be returned. Some people never came back.\nBut it stopped.\nThe boy found the girl sitting on her doorstep, dirty and exhausted. He sat beside her without a word. She leaned her head back and closed her eyes.\nShe never told anyone what she had done. There were no answers given to the village — only the quiet, ordinary fact that the disappearances stopped, and the night sky remained empty, and life continued.\nSome mornings she looked up, just for a moment. Half expecting to see something there.\nThere never was.\nBut sometimes, just before sunrise, when the dark was at its thinnest — she thought she could feel it. The other world. Not gone completely. Just resting on the other side of the light, the way a dream stays with you even after you have forgotten what it was.\nShe never spoke about it.\nShe didn't need to.\n— End —",
