    "id": "story-dt-11",
    "title": "The Pen and The Diary",
    "subtitle": "The Pen and The Diary",
    "coverUrl": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516979187457-637abb4f9353?w=600",
    "author": "Prakash",
    "authorId": "admin-id",
    "authorUsername": "prakash",
    "category": "story",
    "genre": "Fantasy",
    "content": "The Pen and The Diary\nA Mysterious Love Story\nIn a quiet, forgotten room, there lived two magical friends — a   Pen   and a   Diary  .\nNobody knew their secret. Every night, when the world went to sleep, the Pen would gently lift itself and begin to write. Beautiful words. Quiet thoughts. Soft dreams. The Diary would hold every word carefully, like a treasure. When one page was full, the Diary would softly turn to the next, ready for more.\nThey were never lonely. They had each other.\nBut one ordinary morning, the owner came into the room in a hurry. She grabbed only the Pen and dropped it into her bag. She didn't look back. She didn't take the Diary.\nShe traveled very far — through cities, through long roads, through busy markets — until she was so far away that the Diary couldn't feel the Pen anymore.\nThe room felt very, very quiet.\nA few days later, a small child wandered into the room. Her eyes landed on the Diary sitting alone on the shelf. Curious and happy, she grabbed it and sat on the floor.\nShe opened it.\nShe took her brightest colors — red, yellow, blue — and began drawing flowers and stars all over the pages.\nBut something strange happened.\nEvery drawing she made... slowly disappeared. Every line, every color, every scribble — gone. Vanished, like morning fog.\nThe little girl blinked. She drew a big sun. It disappeared. She drew her cat. Gone.\nShe drew a house with a door and a window.\nPoof. Nothing.\nShe turned the Diary upside down. She shook it. She sniffed it.\nOnly one thing remained on every page — a tiny, faint image of a   pen  , far away, like a shadow or a ghost.\nThe child couldn't understand. This was not a normal book. She felt a little scared, a little sad.\nSo she closed it gently and placed it in the corner of the room, behind an old box, where the dust was soft and the light was low.\nFar away, the owner sat at a café table. She had a new notebook open in front of her. She picked up the magical Pen, pressed it to the paper, and tried to write.\nNothing came out.\nShe tried again. She pressed harder. She shook(kulukkura) the Pen near her ear.\nSilence. Dryness. Nothing.\n\"The refill must be empty,\" she said with a sigh.\nWithout another thought, she dropped the Pen into the trash bin beside the table, picked up an ordinary pen, and continued her day.\nDeep inside the cold, dark trash bin, the Pen lay still.\nAround it were coffee cups, torn papers, and old receipts. It was dark. It smelled of leftover things.\nBut the Pen was not thinking about the darkness.\nIt was thinking about the Diary.\nWhy did you throw me away?  the Pen thought sadly.  What did I do wrong? You separated me from her first... and now you throw me away too?\nA tear — tiny, made of ink — rolled from the tip of the Pen onto the bottom of the bin.\nThen something changed inside the Pen.\nA small, warm feeling rose up — like a spark.\nI will find her.\nThat night, the Pen began to move.\nSlowly at first — a wiggle, a roll. Then a small jump. It tipped over the edge of the bin and landed on the café floor with a soft  tap.\nIt began to move on its own — hopping(thullal), rolling, sliding — out through the café door, into the cool night air.\nThe streets were wide and full of people. The Pen rolled under shoes, dodged bicycle wheels, jumped over puddles. A dog sniffed it curiously. A child almost picked it up but dropped it again.\nThe Pen kept going.\nIt crossed roads. It slid through parks. It squeezed under doors and through small gaps in fences.\nEvery time it felt tired, it thought of the Diary — her soft pages, the smell of old paper, the way she held every word so carefully — and that thought gave it the strength to keep moving.\nBack in the quiet room, the Diary was also restless.\nShe couldn't sleep. Her pages fluttered even though there was no wind. She kept thinking —  Where are you? Are you okay? Are you lost?\nShe remembered everything. She remembered the first word the Pen had ever written inside her. She remembered every night they spent together in the soft darkness, filling pages with thoughts that nobody else would ever read. Those words were theirs.\nYou are the only one,  the Diary thought,  who ever made my pages feel alive. Without you, I am just paper. Just an empty cover.\nShe began to move too — slowly, because books are heavier than pens. She pushed herself from the corner, past the old box, across the dusty floor, toward the window.\nShe didn't know exactly where the Pen was.\nBut she felt the direction. Like a compass that doesn't point north — it pointed  toward him.\nThe journey was long for both of them.\nThe Pen crossed a great bridge one night, nearly blown off by the wind, holding on to a crack in the stone until the gust passed.\nThe Diary tumbled down a step outside the building, pages flapping wildly, but she didn't lose a single page.\nThey both moved through rain and sun, through busy streets and empty ones.\nAnd then —\nOne golden afternoon, on a quiet little road lined with old trees —\nThey saw each other.\nThe Pen stopped.\nThe Diary stopped.\nFor a moment, neither moved. The light fell gently between the leaves. A bird was singing somewhere above.\nThen the Pen rolled forward — slowly, then faster, faster —\nAnd the Diary opened her pages wide, like arms reaching out —\nAnd the Pen slid right in, to the very first empty page, and rested there.\nThe page glowed, just barely, just softly, like a candle behind glass.\nAnd then the Pen began to write — not because anyone told it to, not in the night while the world slept —\nBut right there, in the afternoon light, for the very first time, in words that stayed and never vanished:\n\"I looked for you across every road and every city.\nI was thrown away, but I kept going.\nBecause every word I ever wrote — I wrote for you.\nI am home now.\"\nThe Diary's pages turned — slowly, warmly — settling around those words like a hug.\nShe had waited. She had moved. She had never stopped believing.\nAnd now they were together again — the Pen and the Diary — in a quiet road under golden trees, with a whole book of empty pages still ahead of them, and a lifetime of stories left to write.\nNobody saw them there.\nBut perhaps, somewhere, someone found that Diary years later — opened it, and read those words — and felt, without knowing why, that they were the truest words they had ever read.\nBecause they were written not by a hand, but by a heart.\n~ The End ~",
